(make a sirrup; put to your sugar a pint of water; then put them in,) Tj T*
(set them on the fire to boil slowly, till they be clear, skimming them) Tj T*
(often, and they will be very green. Put them up in glasses, and keep) Tj T*
(them for use.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(_To preserve Cherries_.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Take two pounds of cherries, one pound and a half of sugar, half a) Tj T*
(pint of fair water, melt some sugar in it; when it is melted, put in) Tj T*
(your other sugar and your cherries; then boil them softly, till all) Tj T*
(the sugar be melted; then boil them fast, and skim them; take them off) Tj T*
(two or three times and shake them, and put them on again, and let them) Tj T*
(boil fast; and when they are of a good colour, and the sirrup will) Tj T*
(stand, they are boiled enough.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(_To preserve Raspberries_.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Chuse raspberries that are not too ripe, and take the weight of them) Tj T*
(in sugar, wet your sugar with a little water, and put in your berries,) Tj T*
(and let them boil softly; take heed of breaking them; when they are) Tj T*
(clear, take them up, and boil the sirrup till it be thick enough, then) Tj T*
(put them in again; and when they are cold, put them up in glasses.) Tj T*
