(the first sirrup be very thin, a quart of water to a pound of sugar.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(_To pickle or make Mangoes of Melons_.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Take green melons, as many as you please, and make a brine strong) Tj T*
(enough to bear an egg; then pour it boiling hot on the melons, keeping) Tj T*
(them down under the brine; let them stand five or six days; then take) Tj T*
(them out, slit them down on one side, take out all the seeds, scrape) Tj T*
(them well in the inside, and wash them clean with cold water; then) Tj T*
(take a clove of a garlick, a little ginger and nutmeg sliced, and a) Tj T*
(little whole pepper; put all these proportionably into the melons,) Tj T*
(filling them up with mustard-seeds; then lay them in an earthern pot) Tj T*
(with the slit upwards, and take one part of mustard and two parts of) Tj T*
(vinegar, enough to cover them, pouring it upon them scalding hot, and) Tj T*
(keep them close slopped.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(_To pickle Barberries_.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Take of white wine vinegar and water, of each an equal quantity; to) Tj T*
(every quart of this liquor, put in half a pound of cheap sugar, then) Tj T*
(pick the worst of your barberries and put into this liquor, and the) Tj T*
(best into glasses; then boil your pickle with the worst of your) Tj T*
(barberries, and skim it very clean, boil it till it looks of a fine) Tj T*
(colour, then let it stand to be cold, before you strain it; then) Tj T*
(strain it through a cloth, wringing it to get all the colour you can) Tj T*
(from the barberries; let it stand to cool and settle, then pour it) Tj T*
(clear into the glasses; in a little of the pickle, boil a little) Tj T*
(fennel; when cold, put a little bit at the top of the pot or glass,) Tj T*
