(_To boil Asparagus_.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(First cut the white ends off about six inches from the head, and) Tj T*
(scrape them from the green part downward very clean, as you scrape) Tj T*
(them, throw them into a pan of clear water, and after a little) Tj T*
(soaking, tie them up in small even bundles, when your water boils, put) Tj T*
(them in, and boil them up quick; but by over boiling they will lose) Tj T*
(their heads; cut a slice of bread, for a toast, and toast it brown on) Tj T*
(both sides; when your asparagus is done, take it up carefully; dip the) Tj T*
(toast in the asparagus water, and lay it in the bottom of your dish;) Tj T*
(then lay the heads of the asparagus on it, with the white ends) Tj T*
(outwards; pour a little melted butter over the heads; cut an orange) Tj T*
(into small pieces, and stick them between for garnish.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(_To boil Cabbage_.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(If your cabbage is large, cut it into quarters; if small, cut it in) Tj T*
(halves; let your water boil, then put in a little salt, and next your) Tj T*
(cabbage with a little more salt upon it; make your water boil as soon) Tj T*
(as possible, and when the stalk is tender, take up your cabbage into a) Tj T*
(cullender, or sieve, that the water may drain off, and send it to) Tj T*
(table as hot as you can. Savoys are dressed in the same manner.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(_For brewing Spruce Beer_.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Take four ounces of hops, let them boil half an hour in one gallon of) Tj T*
(water, strain the hop water then add sixteen gallons of warm water,) Tj T*
(two gallons of molasses, eight ounces of essence of spruce, dissolved) Tj T*
(in one quart of water, put it in a clean cask, then shake it well) Tj T*
(together, add half a pint of emptins, then let it stand and work one) Tj T*
(week, if very warm weather less time will do, when it is drawn off to) Tj T*
(bottle, add one spoonful of molasses to every bottle.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(_Emptins_.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Take a handful of hops and about three quarts of water, let it boil) Tj T*
(about fifteen minutes, then make a thickening as you do for starch,) Tj T*
(strain the liquor, when cold put a little emptins to work them, they) Tj T*
(will keep well cork'd in a bottle five or six weeks.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
